Health monitoring example
=========================

Shows three ARINC 653 services and how a partition learns why it was
started:

  GET_PARTITION_STATUS        partition identifier, period, duration, lock
                              level, operating mode, start condition and
                              number of cores.
  REPORT_APPLICATION_MESSAGE  sends a message to the health monitor, which
                              logs it with the partition name; the process
                              continues.
  CREATE_SAMPLING_PORT        creates a sampling port; the arguments must
                              match the configuration, and the refresh
                              period decides when a message is INVALID.

Partitions
----------

P1 (writer) is started three times. Each time it calls GET_PARTITION_STATUS
and acts on the start condition:

  NORMAL_START          the module has just started. P1 reports a 4-byte
                        status code (REPORT_APPLICATION_MESSAGE sends exactly
                        LENGTH bytes; no NUL is needed) and restarts itself
                        with SET_PARTITION_MODE(COLD_START).
  PARTITION_RESTART     P1 restarted itself. It raises an application error;
                        P1 has no error handler process, so the health
                        monitor applies the HM table in P1/config.xml:
                        APPLICATION_ERROR -> COLD_START.
  HM_PARTITION_RESTART  the health monitor restarted P1. It first calls
                        CREATE_SAMPLING_PORT with a size that doesn't match
                        the configuration (INVALID_CONFIG), then with the
                        right one, and starts a writer process that writes
                        six readings to SP_OUT, one every 500 ms, then stops.

P2 (reader) creates destination port SP_IN with a refresh period of 1 s and
reads it every 500 ms. While P1 writes, readings are VALID; about 1 s after
the last write they become INVALID, and P2 reports the stale data to the
health monitor once.

Build and run
-------------

From the top-level directory:

    make health-monitoring                      # x86_64
    make BSP=arm64-qemu health-monitoring       # arm64

    qemu-system-x86_64 -vga none -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel examples/health-monitoring/build/x86_64-qemu/pok.elf
    qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt -cpu cortex-a53 -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel examples/health-monitoring/build/arm64-qemu/pok.elf

Expected output (shortened; "[HM]" lines come from the kernel's health
monitor, "[KERNEL]" from the HM recovery action):

    P1: partition 1 started: start condition NORMAL_START, mode COLD_START,
        period 60 ms, duration 30 ms, lock level 1, 1 core(s)
    [HM] Application message from partition P1: S001
    [HM] Application message from partition P1: first start, restarting the partition
    P1: partition 1 started: start condition PARTITION_RESTART, mode COLD_START, ...
    [HM] Application message from partition P1: restarted by SET_PARTITION_MODE, raising an application error
    [KERNEL] Restarting partition P1 due to: Application Error
    P1: partition 1 started: start condition HM_PARTITION_RESTART, mode COLD_START, ...
    [HM] Application message from partition P1: restarted by the health monitor, starting the writer
    P1: CREATE_SAMPLING_PORT(SP_OUT, size 64): INVALID_CONFIG (configured size is 32)
    P1: CREATE_SAMPLING_PORT(SP_OUT, size 32): NO_ERROR
    P1: wrote "reading #1"
    P2: partition 2 started, start condition NORMAL_START
    P2: CREATE_SAMPLING_PORT(SP_IN, refresh 1000 ms): NO_ERROR
    P2: read "reading #1" (11 bytes): VALID
    ...
    P1: wrote "reading #6"
    [HM] Application message from partition P1: writer stopped, no new readings on SP_OUT
    P2: read "reading #6" (11 bytes): VALID
    P2: read "reading #6" (11 bytes): INVALID, older than the refresh period
    [HM] Application message from partition P2: stale reading on SP_IN
    ...
    P2: done
